/* Lovable learnings: distilled from uploads/kortx-learnings-export.json (215 published KORTX pages,
   Apr to Nov 2026). LOVABLE_GUIDE goes in the static system prompt; relevantPages() picks the
   closest published pages by archetype, vertical and client for the per-request context.
   Regenerate when a new export is uploaded. */
export const LOVABLE_GUIDE = "LOVABLE LEARNINGS (house patterns distilled from 215 published KORTX pages, Apr to Nov 2026: media proposals, digital advertising audits, competitive intelligence, growth playbooks, rich media storyboards, partnership POVs and seasonal packages). They are CONTENT REFERENCE ONLY: use them for what to say, section order and copy moves. They never set the look. Every page keeps the KORTX Creative Lab look and feel exactly as built here (Creative Playground ground and glows, Inter, KORTX components, the fixed catalog blocks and containers, liquid-glass tab menu, scroll reveals). Never copy their layout, colors, fonts, light or dark theme toggles, icons or markup. The HOUSE RULES still win (no em or en dashes, no rates or fees, no DSP names, geo-targeted).\n\n1. PICK THE ARCHETYPE FIRST, from the brief and the title, then follow its spine:\n- Media Proposal (VA DMV, Idaho Lottery, HQRx, Versiti, MSU Athletics, Alson Jewelers). Hero: client, campaign accent, ONE strategy sentence (\"A statewide campaign built to <outcome>, with <mechanism>.\"), four pills (flight · \"$X Total Investment\" · geo · \"n Channels · n Core Audiences\"). Spine: Overview (\"What are we solving for?\" opens with a reframe: \"<Client> is not solving an awareness problem. <the real problem>.\"; 3 to 4 headline stats with one-line notes; \"The strategic tension\") → KORTX Intelligence (market analysis: 4 sourced numbers; \"Market trends\" and \"Audience trends\": 4 short declarative headlines each, one supporting line each) → Audience (three steps \"Define the <buyers> · Map them to channels · <Activate / Let the offer sell>\"; 2 to 3 personas; buyer → channel fit with High / Moderate / Low fit and a \"Takeaway\") → Media plan (Objective · Audience · Flight · Budget · Geography facts, then the plan table with a Total row; budget tiers when the brief gives options, one marked Recommended) → Audience & data strategy (\"How we find them.\", targeting methodology, taxonomy examples marked \"Draft, pending segment sign off at kickoff\") → Creative → Measurement in Kampus → closing.\n- Digital Advertising Audit, Executive Snapshot (Orkin, Spectrum, Shiner, Chicago Fire, Gong cha, Vimergy, Thrasher). Reader's guide Verified · Inferred · Assumed (or Not observable) as confidence chips. Quality score out of 100 with tier and \"Ceiling with KORTX\", five scored rows (Brand and creative strength /20 · Programmatic sophistication /25 · Audience and data strategy /25 · Measurement and conversion /20 · Website and CRO /10) plus \"What matters most\". Numbered parts (\"Industry · Section 1\", \"Audience · Section 2\", Personas, Data Partners as Strength / Opportunity / To validate, Media Mix as Channel · Prior state · KORTX improvement · Basis), then Strategy, Measurement, Creative Mocks, Win Theme, Competitive Intel. Shorter audits use two tabs: \"What we found\" · \"What we'd do\".\n- Competitive Media Spend Analysis (Bumble Bee Blinds, Cedarhurst, Crispy Cajun, Wipfli, AmFirst). Hero of 4 stat tiles (competitors analyzed, tiers, client's verified spend, digital share). Confidence key High · Medium · Directional. Numbered chapters \"01 · The Field\" (competitive set) · \"02 · The Numbers\" (spend table) · \"03 · Visualized\" (mix bars) · \"04 · Capability Map\" (tactics matrix, observed vs modeled) · \"05 · What It Means\" (each finding: evidence label Observed / Modeled / Mixed, a one-line headline, then Finding · Implication · KORTX Angle) · \"06 · The Recommendation\". Executive summary and Methodology close it.\n- Vertical Growth Playbook (Home Services, Retail, Education, Healthcare, Senior Living, Sports, Automotive, Travel, Financial Services). Title \"The <Vertical> Growth Playbook\"; lede ends \"Not a pitch of what we could do, a walk-through of exactly how we do it.\"; \"What's inside\" 01 to 06: Why KORTX · Proof & Case Study · The Playbook (five selectable plays) · <Audience> Audiences · Live Creative · Measurement & Kampus. Full-funnel allocation in four stages (Awareness & Trust · Consideration · Conversion · Retention & Referral), each with a role line, $ and channels; key conversion actions; benchmark ranges table; \"Four data layers, one ready <customer>\"; taxonomy paths written \"Purchase Intent › Home Services › Emergency Repair\".\n- Signal-based Rich Media Storyboards (Orkin, GECU, Spectrum, First Federal Lakewood). Two-line hero (\"Big capability. / Right here at home.\"), 4 brand fact tiles, \"How the concepts differ\" table (Concept · Primary signal · What changes · Best use), then one block per concept: headline, three steps, signal tags, \"Why it can work:\".\n- Partnership / Agency POV (ROI Revolution, BxB Media, Horizon, Colling Media, Embedded Agency). The Challenge (three numbered pressures, a title and one line each) → what KORTX brings → \"Four ways in\" → the Loop (Pre-Sale Support · Strategy & Creative · Execution · Data & Analytics · Renewal & Growth) → where we start / next steps.\n- Seasonal Package offers (FNBO Holiday, Amazon Ads). Sourced stat tiles (source and date in the note), market and consumer trends, a phased calendar (Prime · Peak · Carry · Reset with key dates), \"Five packages. One season.\" (each: flight, channels, targeting, primary KPI), Compare, Why KORTX, Next steps.\n- Creative Showcase. Hero \"Creative that earns attention.\"; \"Why we built this for you\"; \"Experience the creative.\" with the formats (Social Media Extension, Rich Media, Online Video, Connected TV, Digital Out of Home); resources; closing \"Let's keep the conversation going.\"\n- Market Intelligence / RFP insights (Southcentral CTC, Kentucky nonprofit RFP). Geography and county profiles, audience segments, media consumption, traditional vs digital, tactic fit, executive summary, methodology.\n\n2. COPY MOVES THAT REPEAT ACROSS ALL OF THEM\n- Two-line contrast headlines: \"More than a media buy. / A Spartan revenue engine.\", \"From reach to donor applications. / A conversion plan for 2027.\", \"Local relevance. / National trust.\"\n- Section heads are short declarative claims, not labels: \"Supply is fixed, so velocity is the only variable.\", \"Two buyers, one game.\", \"A loop, not a handoff.\"\n- Every research figure carries its source; every inferred figure carries its confidence label. Illustrative numbers say so.\n- Each section ends on a takeaway that names the client (\"Takeaway · \", \"What this means for <client>\", \"Why it can work:\").\n- Personas get a memorable name plus demo line (\"Trigger-Moment Tara · A30-55 · homeowner · $75K-$150K HHI\") and a one-line \"Creative job\".\n- Concepts pair Target audience · Campaign goal · Creative messaging · Media mix / channels, and name the art direction the image zone needs.\n- Hero actions are \"See the recommendation\" and \"Jump to the media plan\" (or \"See the plan\" / \"View creative concepts\").\n\n3. EXEMPLARS: the Builder context may include RELEVANT PAST PAGES (outlines of the closest published pages). Match their section order, density and headline style; never copy their client facts, numbers or names into this client's page.";

